::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.01] Passage: The story repeats the image of a cracked bell each time the town ignores a warning. What does the repeated bell most likely add to the text? {
=It adds a symbolic pattern showing ignored danger.#Correct. The repeated cracked bell deepens the warning motif.
~It proves the town has no conflict.#The repeated warning suggests conflict.
~It is only a random object.#Its repetition makes it meaningful.
~It removes the need for analysis.#The detail invites analysis.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Passage: One text shows a traveler leaving home for work. Another shows a student leaving home for school. Both characters feel hope and sadness. What theme connects the texts? {
~Leaving home is always a mistake.#This is too absolute.
=Change can bring opportunity and loss at the same time.#Correct. This theme fits both passages.
~School and work are identical.#They are different situations.
~Hope and sadness cannot appear together.#The passage says both appear.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: Dana called the old factory a scar. Luis called it an open room waiting for desks. What does the juxtaposition reveal? {
~Both characters see the factory the same way.#The descriptions contrast.
~The factory has no meaning.#Both descriptions assign meaning.
=Dana sees damage, while Luis sees possibility.#Correct. The contrast defines perspective.
~Luis dislikes schools.#The desks suggest a school possibility.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.04] Context: A poem is written during a period when families waited for wartime letters. Poem: Each morning, the mailbox opens / its small empty mouth. What issue does the poem reflect? {
~A celebration of mailboxes only.#The context points beyond the object.
~A lesson about building roads.#Roads are not discussed.
~A recipe for writing letters.#The poem is about waiting, not writing instructions.
=Wartime uncertainty and waiting for news.#Correct. The empty mailbox reflects waiting and uncertainty.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.09] Passage: The city handed every citizen a paper crown and called them free. What allegorical idea is most likely suggested? {
=A society may offer symbols of power without real power.#Correct. Paper crowns suggest appearance without substance.
~Paper is stronger than metal.#The material is symbolic, not a strength comparison.
~Every citizen becomes a ruler in fact.#The phrase "called them free" suggests questioning the claim.
~Crowns have no possible meaning.#The crown is the central symbol.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write as public rail lines expand. Author A says rails connect towns to medicine and letters. Author B says rails bring buyers who may displace families. How do the authors differ? {
~Author A discusses weather, while Author B discusses poetry.#Both discuss rail expansion.
=Author A emphasizes connection, while Author B emphasizes displacement.#Correct. This compares their reasoning.
~Both authors reject rails for the same reason.#Author A sees benefits.
~Author B focuses only on train speed.#Author B focuses on displacement and ownership.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A school garden is replaced by a parking lot. One character sees the garden as community memory. Another sees the parking lot as needed access. Which plan best establishes multiple perspectives? {
~Describe only the color of the pavement.#This does not show both perspectives.
~Write that everyone feels the same way.#This removes multiple perspectives.
=Show one character saving seed labels and another watching an elder struggle to walk from far away.#Correct. These details reveal different values.
~List unrelated events from the school year.#This would not focus the narrative.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.V.03] Passage: The judge's apology was a velvet curtain over a broken window. What does velvet suggest in context? {
~A rough and ugly object.#Velvet suggests softness or beauty.
~A literal window repair.#The phrase is metaphorical.
~A legal document.#No document is mentioned.
=Something smooth or attractive that may hide damage.#Correct. The connotation supports the metaphor.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Passage: The old map showed only roads already traveled, so Mira drew a line toward the blank mountains. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~Mira refuses to use maps because mountains are blank.#This misstates the action.
~The map shows every possible route.#The map shows only roads already traveled.
=Mira challenges limited choices by marking a new possible path.#Correct. This captures the action and meaning.
~Mira erases every road on the map.#The passage says she draws a line.
}

::ENG3_U02_PRE_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence is conventionally correct? {
~Mira drew a new path it crossed the mountains.#This is a run-on.
~mira drew a new path across the mountains.#The name should be capitalized.
~Mira drew, a new path across the mountains.#The comma incorrectly separates the verb from its object.
=Mira drew a new path across the mountains.#Correct. The sentence uses standard capitalization and punctuation.
}
